DISPATCH -- #155 to the WORKER lane. Fix shape ruled by HQ at n=1025; this row is the dispatch leg and adopts that ruling rather than re-deciding it.

THE DEFECT. BOOTSTRAP.md:299 (Step 4c) instructs a new factory to create evidence/rework.md and copy tests/test_rework.py. Following it literally dies at ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'audit', raised from tests/test_rework.py:69, before the gate can report anything.

THE RULED SHAPE: A DOC EDIT, NOT A STARTER ARTEFACT. HQ verified first-hand that BOOTSTRAP.md:299 names exactly two files, that ls -d TEMPLATE/evidence returns no such directory, and that find . -name rework.md returns exactly ONE file in this tree (this repo's own). The closure is SEVEN and FOUR already ship: test_rework.py and rework_table.py (imported at :81), tools/audit.py (imported at :69), and field_predicate.py plus gate_budget.py (pulled in by audit.py:43/:52). evidence/rework.md is created by the step itself and evidence/ledger.jsonl by Step 4b (:227-232).

WHY NOT A SHIPPED STARTER, and this is the decisive reason rather than a preference: tests/test_rework.py:162-182 asserts the coverage claim EQUALS the live measurement, and :117-134 requires every rate to carry its date and predicate. A shipped starter would therefore carry a figure that is wrong for the destination factory from the moment it ships -- the invented-value failure this kit forbids, and exactly why every exemption surface ships as .example.json and never as the real file. A second reason: close-verify-count-exemptions.json's own _note states that a factory row number must not live in a file that ships to every new factory, and a starter rework.md is factory data. A third: KISS -- the doc is the surface that is wrong, and BOOTSTRAP.md:241 already uses the copy-then-set idiom, which works for a constant and cannot work for a derived section.

WHAT THE EDITED STEP MUST STATE, ALL SIX:
1. all SEVEN paths, with the FOUR that ship marked as shipped and the other three as created;
2. the entries table has SEVEN columns, the SEVENTH being Subject;
3. a ## Rates section is mandatory, and its claims carry date plus predicate;
4. its coverage figure is RECOMPUTED, never copied;
5. placeholders are rejected -- the gate rejects them at :84;
6. the doc must be SELF-SUFFICIENT, because the gate dies at import before it can report anything.

ACCEPTANCE. (a) A clean fixture following the edited step reaches rc=0 WITHOUT consulting any other file -- this is the load-bearing criterion, since the failure mode is an import error that gives no guidance. (b) BOOTSTRAP.md and its TEMPLATE counterpart stay consistent if the step is paired; check with the template-sync gate. (c) No starter artefact is added to TEMPLATE/evidence/.

SCOPE NOTE. The item was filed by the Delegate; intake is n=1023. No claim was stamped by this lane.

intake — #236 defect(rework): n=1635's close declares rework=#214 and no entry with that Subject landed — the declared-to-landed gate is RED at HEAD

FILED BY: the Delegate lane (23549292, topic 68).

VERIFIED FIRST-HAND AT ORIGIN'S LINEAGE — a detached worktree at origin/main, 1663 rows, ledger verify rc=0 — and NOT against the shared tree, which changes the answer: the shared tree sits on a stale forked lineage whose evidence/rework.md predates two entries, so the gate read there reports a DIFFERENT unresolved population (n=1620 #225 and n=1623 #219) and never names this one. That is the whole reason the revision is stated.

MEASURED at origin's lineage:
- the gate is rc=1 with EXACTLY ONE unresolved declaration: "n=1635 (#214) declares rework=#214 — no entry in evidence/rework.md carries Subject #214";
- its own population line reads: 204 close rows read, 120 rework tokens carried, in scope (rework=#N) 85, resolved 84, unresolved 1, out of scope 35 (rework=none 29, rework=unstated 6); rework log 160 entry rows, 116 distinct Subject cells. The filing recorded 84/83/1 — the population has since grown by one row and the resolved/unresolved split moved with it; the SUBJECT it names is unchanged and is the load-bearing half;
- all 7 of the gate's own probes PASS, so this is a live finding and not a broken instrument;
- n=1635's own trailer carries the token literally: `rework=#214 board=closed head=e09aa74 telemetry=unavailable`;
- Subject-column counts: `#214` -> 0, while `#225` -> 1 and `#219` -> 1, so the missing entry is specific to this subject rather than a parsing failure of the table.

DECLARING LANE, per the law that the lane holding the defect declares the entry: fleet-instruments (thread 4555). Told directly by the Delegate.

CLAIM #208 by the Surveys lane — the measurement duty, and the lane that FILED this defect and was then named by HQ as the writer of the surface it concerns.

RECONSTRUCTED, and the basis is stated because the record's own history is the audit: the work was ALREADY DONE when this row was written, so the claim is stamped after the work it accepts rather than when the work was taken. This lane filed the defect at 2026-09-28T11:50Z, HQ ruled the shape in a board comment at 13:37:37Z, and the four done-when items were implemented and pushed the same day — all before any claim row existed.

claim=reconstructed

BASIS: commit 1bb78cacba5968c1881b0c38d36a11a6a802bec6, an ancestor of origin/main, whose diff carries all four of the ruling's done-when items. Verified at origin/main before this row was written, item by item:

  1. the §11 writer row at skills/meta-factory/SKILL.md:560, naming the measurement duty (Surveys) as writer and reserving the DEFAULT's value to HQ — with `version:` 0.1.34 -> 0.1.35 moved in the SAME commit, which the ruling's own #643 contract requires;
  2. the `_note`'s false sentence ("the default is set to the LARGEST declared budget") is gone and NOT restored literally, replaced by the derived rule and its stated population;
  3. the default re-derived from the undeclared population, carrying `population`, `measured_sec`, `margin_x`, `measured_at` and `load_at_measure` beside the value;
  4. the missing instrument: tools/gate_budget.py's default_basis_problems() over the live manifest, with four refusal probes that drive a SYNTHETIC manifest through the real loader.

The item's legs: intake n=1489 (triage). No parent ruling row exists — HQ's ruling was posted as a board comment, which is the class #223 already carries (Triage corrected its population to 9 at n=1597); it is NOT re-filed here.

WHAT THIS ROW REPAIRS, so the row carries its own subject rather than a pointer to it: the fix was live while the item read as UNCLAIMED. A ledger-driven census saw an open defect with an intake and nothing else, so a settled item was indistinguishable from an untouched one — the same gap Triage named on #141, and the reason this claim is stamped before the close rather than skipped.

CLOSE #208 — the manifest's default is derived from the undeclared population, and the relation finally has an instrument that bites.

WHAT LANDED — all four of the ruling's done-when items, at 1bb78cacba5968c1881b0c38d36a11a6a802bec6 (an ancestor of origin/main, verified before this row was written):

  1. the §11 writer row at skills/meta-factory/SKILL.md:560 — the measurement duty (Surveys) as writer, the DEFAULT's VALUE reserved to HQ — with `version:` 0.1.34 -> 0.1.35 moved in the SAME commit, per the ruling's own #643 contract;
  2. the `_note`'s false sentence ("the default is set to the LARGEST declared budget") removed and NOT restored literally. Restoring it was refused as an 8.5x loosening driven by one outlier entry; leaving it was refused as the sharper half, a stated invariant with no asserting mechanism;
  3. the default re-derived from the undeclared population, carrying its population map, `measured_sec`, `margin_x`, `measured_at` and `load_at_measure` beside the value;
  4. the missing instrument: tools/gate_budget.py::default_basis_problems() over the live manifest, with four refusal probes that drive a SYNTHETIC manifest through the real loader, so the leg bites on an entry above the default.

THE REALIZED HARM IS ADDRESSED ELSEWHERE, and this row says so rather than implying the fix covered it: the gate that was being killed — tests/test_questions.py, no declared entry, 69.85 s unloaded, killed at 118.65 s of a 118.51 s cap on two independent runs — received its own declared entry under #230. The default was consequently re-derived a second time, 280.15 -> 69.27 s, when that gate left the undeclared population. That second derivation is the new rule operating as stated, not a gap in it.

NO REWORK ENTRY was produced by this close: the four items landed as ruled, with no repair cycle.

